WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.629
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.929 --> 00:00:09.071
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.371 --> 00:00:16.354
Unit thirty-four: The Science of Learning. In this unit, you will understand how the brain learns most effectively.

4
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:18.537
metacognition.

5
00:00:19.237 --> 00:00:22.792
metacognition means thinking about your own thinking and learning.

6
00:00:23.092 --> 00:00:26.659
Metacognition helps students plan and check their learning.

7
00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:30.441
Your turn. Say: metacognition.

8
00:00:33.141 --> 00:00:34.328
metacognition.

9
00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:36.852
spaced repetition.

10
00:00:37.552 --> 00:00:42.106
spaced repetition means reviewing information at increasing intervals.

11
00:00:42.406 --> 00:00:47.238
Spaced repetition apps help learners remember vocabulary.

12
00:00:47.938 --> 00:00:51.368
Your turn. Say: spaced repetition.

13
00:00:54.068 --> 00:00:55.371
spaced repetition.

14
00:00:56.383 --> 00:00:57.849
retrieval practice.

15
00:00:58.549 --> 00:01:03.172
retrieval practice means learning by trying to recall information from memory.

16
00:01:03.472 --> 00:01:07.086
Self-testing is a powerful form of retrieval practice.

17
00:01:07.786 --> 00:01:10.879
Your turn. Say: retrieval practice.

18
00:01:13.579 --> 00:01:15.161
retrieval practice.

19
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:17.418
cognitive load.

20
00:01:18.118 --> 00:01:22.405
cognitive load means the amount of mental effort a task requires.

21
00:01:22.705 --> 00:01:25.622
Too much cognitive load makes learning difficult.

22
00:01:26.322 --> 00:01:29.833
Your turn. Say: cognitive load.

23
00:01:32.533 --> 00:01:33.755
cognitive load.

24
00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:36.030
working memory.

25
00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:41.934
working memory means the brain's short-term space for holding and using information.

26
00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:46.230
Working memory can hold only a few items at once.

27
00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:49.710
Your turn. Say: working memory.

28
00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:53.492
working memory.

29
00:01:54.483 --> 00:01:55.902
neuroplasticity.

30
00:01:56.602 --> 00:02:01.551
neuroplasticity means the brain's ability to change and form new connections.

31
00:02:01.851 --> 00:02:05.174
Neuroplasticity means we can learn at any age.

32
00:02:05.874 --> 00:02:09.339
Your turn. Say: neuroplasticity.

33
00:02:12.039 --> 00:02:13.412
neuroplasticity.

34
00:02:14.417 --> 00:02:15.859
growth trajectory.

35
00:02:16.559 --> 00:02:20.346
growth trajectory means the path of a learner's development over time.

36
00:02:20.646 --> 00:02:25.850
Her growth trajectory accelerated after she changed methods.

37
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:30.213
Your turn. Say: growth trajectory.

38
00:02:32.913 --> 00:02:34.239
growth trajectory.

39
00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.356
interleaving.

40
00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:41.052
interleaving means mixing different topics or skills during practice.

41
00:02:41.352 --> 00:02:44.420
Interleaving maths problems improves understanding.

42
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.795
Your turn. Say: interleaving.

43
00:02:50.495 --> 00:02:51.578
interleaving.

44
00:02:52.583 --> 00:02:54.827
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:55.127 --> 00:02:59.773
Ms. Sarah, I read my notes again and again, but I still forget.

46
00:03:01.083 --> 00:03:06.136
Rereading is the method on which most students rely, yet it's the least effective.

47
00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:09.603
Really? What should I do instead?

48
00:03:10.917 --> 00:03:17.400
Retrieval practice: test yourself. Recalling information strengthens memory far more.

49
00:03:18.717 --> 00:03:22.272
And spaced repetition, which my vocabulary app uses?

50
00:03:23.583 --> 00:03:30.021
Exactly. Reviewing at increasing intervals is the approach for which the evidence is strongest.

51
00:03:31.317 --> 00:03:33.908
Is it true that the brain can change at any age?

52
00:03:35.217 --> 00:03:42.559
Yes. Neuroplasticity, the discovery of which transformed education, shows learning never stops.

53
00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:47.754
Grammar focus: Advanced relative clauses (extended).

54
00:03:48.054 --> 00:03:51.806
Formal relative clauses put the preposition before which or whom.

55
00:03:52.106 --> 00:03:54.791
It's the method on which most students rely.

56
00:03:55.091 --> 00:04:00.782
Neuroplasticity, the discovery of which transformed education, shows learning never stops.

57
00:04:01.082 --> 00:04:07.970
These structures are typical of academic writing; in speech, we usually move the preposition to the end.

58
00:04:08.983 --> 00:04:11.961
Now read and listen. Learning How to Learn.

59
00:04:12.261 --> 00:04:16.582
Many students spend hours studying, yet remember surprisingly little.

60
00:04:17.883 --> 00:04:23.528
Cognitive scientists have identified techniques that make learning far more efficient.

61
00:04:24.817 --> 00:04:30.671
Retrieval practice, in which learners test themselves, consistently outperforms rereading.

62
00:04:31.983 --> 00:04:37.745
Spaced repetition, the effectiveness of which is supported by decades of research, prevents forgetting.

63
00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:45.241
Interleaving different topics may feel harder, but it improves long-term understanding.

64
00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:52.114
Teachers are also advised to manage cognitive load, since working memory is limited.

65
00:04:53.417 --> 00:04:57.994
Metacognition helps learners monitor what they know and adjust their strategies.

66
00:04:59.283 --> 00:05:04.383
Thanks to neuroplasticity, improvement is possible at every age and stage of life.

67
00:05:05.683 --> 00:05:08.405
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:08.705 --> 00:05:10.937
Thinking about your own thinking is blank.

69
00:05:11.237 --> 00:05:17.212
A, metacognition. B, interleaving. C, neuroplasticity.

70
00:05:21.217 --> 00:05:24.261
The answer is A: metacognition.

71
00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:27.738
This is the book blank I told you.

72
00:05:28.038 --> 00:05:31.715
A, which. B, about which. C, of whom.

73
00:05:35.717 --> 00:05:38.750
The answer is B: about which.

74
00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:43.678
The researcher, blank I worked, was very kind.

75
00:05:43.978 --> 00:05:47.898
A, with whom. B, with who. C, whom with.

76
00:05:51.883 --> 00:05:54.475
The answer is A: with whom.

77
00:05:55.983 --> 00:05:59.562
Learning by recalling information from memory is blank.

78
00:05:59.862 --> 00:06:06.778
A, retrieval practice. B, spaced repetition. C, cognitive load.

79
00:06:10.783 --> 00:06:14.339
The answer is A: retrieval practice.

80
00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:19.905
According to the text, what consistently outperforms rereading?

81
00:06:20.205 --> 00:06:25.065
A, interleaving. B, retrieval practice. C, highlighting.

82
00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.234
The answer is B: retrieval practice.

83
00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:39.630
Now it's your turn. Part one: How could students in Cambodia study more effectively?

84
00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:51.318
Part two: Recommend two techniques, explain the science, and give an example.

85
00:06:57.618 --> 00:07:11.300
I'd recommend retrieval practice and spaced repetition, both of which are supported by strong evidence. Instead of rereading notes, students could test themselves with flashcards, reviewing them after one day, three days and a week.

86
00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:13.832
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:14.817 --> 00:07:20.209
Great work today! You learned eight new words and advanced relative clauses (extended).

88
00:07:20.509 --> 00:07:24.566
Today's value is pride in learning. Be proud of what you learned.

89
00:07:24.866 --> 00:07:28.505
See you in Unit thirty-five. Goodbye!

90
00:07:28.805 --> 00:07:32.537
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

